  {
   "id": "greedy",
   "title": "Greedy versus lazy",
   "topic": "3. Quantifiers",
   "blurb": "Why <.+> swallows the whole line and how to fix it.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "A greedy pattern grabs too much",
     "text": "The goal is to find each tag. <.+> reads as: a <, then one or more of any character, then a >. The match is the whole string.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "<.+>",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "<b>bold</b> and <i>it</i>",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          25,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": "1 match"
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Why: + takes as much as it can",
     "text": "Quantifiers are greedy. The engine lets .+ swallow everything first, then gives characters back one at a time until the rest of the pattern (a >) can match. The first character it gives back is the final > of the string, so one huge match results.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "Attempt",
       "What .+ has taken",
       "Can > come next?"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "1",
        "everything to the end of the string",
        "no: nothing is left to match >"
       ],
       [
        "2",
        "all but the last character",
        "the last character is >: yes, the match ends there"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        1,
        2
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "A lazy pattern takes as little as it can",
     "text": "Adding ? after the + makes it lazy: .+? takes one character, tries the rest of the pattern, and only takes more if that fails. Now each tag is its own match.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "<.+?>",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "<b>bold</b> and <i>it</i>",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          3,
          0
         ],
         [
          7,
          11,
          0
         ],
         [
          16,
          19,
          0
         ],
         [
          21,
          25,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": "4 matches"
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Often better: say what you mean",
     "text": "[^>]+ means one or more characters that are not >. It cannot run past the end of a tag, so no laziness is needed and it is faster for the engine.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "<[^>]+>",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "<b>bold</b> and <i>it</i>",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          3,
          0
         ],
         [
          7,
          11,
          0
         ],
         [
          16,
          19,
          0
         ],
         [
          21,
          25,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": "4 matches"
       }
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "anchors",
   "title": "search, match, fullmatch and anchors",
   "topic": "4. Anchors, boundaries and the match functions",
   "blurb": "Which function answers which question, and the trailing-newline trap.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Searching finds it anywhere",
     "text": "re.search looks for the pattern anywhere in the string. \\d{3} finds three digits inside 12345 and inside a123b, which is fine for finding but wrong for checking a whole value.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "\\d{3}",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "123",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          3,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       },
       {
        "text": "12345",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          3,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       },
       {
        "text": "a123b",
        "spans": [
         [
          1,
          4,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Three functions, three questions",
     "text": "re.search: is it anywhere? re.match: does it match at the start? re.fullmatch: does the whole string match? For validation you almost always want fullmatch.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "re.search",
       "re.match",
       "re.fullmatch"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "'123'",
        "'123'",
        "'123'",
        "'123'"
       ],
       [
        "'12345'",
        "'123'",
        "'123'",
        "None"
       ],
       [
        "'a123b'",
        "'123'",
        "None",
        "None"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        0,
        3
       ],
       [
        1,
        3
       ],
       [
        2,
        3
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Or anchor the pattern",
     "text": "^ matches only at the start and $ only at the end. ^\\d{3}$ with re.search behaves like fullmatch for these strings.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "^\\d{3}$",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "123",
        "spans": [
         [
          0,
          3,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       },
       {
        "text": "12345",
        "spans": [],
        "note": ""
       },
       {
        "text": "a123b",
        "spans": [],
        "note": ""
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "The trailing-newline trap",
     "text": "$ also matches just before a final newline, so ^\\d{3}$ accepts '123\\n'. \\Z means the very end of the string, and re.fullmatch has no such trap.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "re.search(r'^\\d{3}$')",
       "re.search(r'^\\d{3}\\Z')",
       "re.fullmatch(r'\\d{3}')"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "'123'",
        "'123'",
        "'123'",
        "'123'"
       ],
       [
        "'123\\n'",
        "'123'",
        "None",
        "None"
       ],
       [
        "'1234'",
        "None",
        "None",
        "None"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        1,
        1
       ]
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "groups",
   "title": "Groups: capture, name and rearrange",
   "topic": "5. Groups, alternation and backreferences",
   "blurb": "Pull a date apart, name the pieces, and put them back in a new order.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Without groups: just the whole match",
     "text": "This finds both dates, but they come back as plain strings.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "Due 2024-03-15 and 2025-12-01",
        "spans": [
         [
          4,
          14,
          0
         ],
         [
          19,
          29,
          0
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Parentheses capture parts",
     "text": "Each (...) is a numbered group, counted by its opening parenthesis from the left. Orange is the whole match, green the year, blue the month, purple the day.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "match",
      "pattern": "(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})",
      "flags": "",
      "lines": [
       {
        "text": "Due 2024-03-15 and 2025-12-01",
        "spans": [
         [
          4,
          14,
          0
         ],
         [
          4,
          8,
          1
         ],
         [
          9,
          11,
          2
         ],
         [
          12,
          14,
          3
         ],
         [
          19,
          29,
          0
         ],
         [
          19,
          23,
          1
         ],
         [
          24,
          26,
          2
         ],
         [
          27,
          29,
          3
         ]
        ],
        "note": ""
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "findall now returns the groups",
     "text": "With groups in the pattern, re.findall returns a tuple of the groups for each match and no longer returns the whole match. This surprises everyone once.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "Call",
       "Returns"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "re.findall(r'\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}', text)",
        "['2024-03-15', '2025-12-01']"
       ],
       [
        "re.findall(r'(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})', text)",
        "[('2024', '03', '15'), ('2025', '12', '01')]"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        1,
        1
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Name the groups",
     "text": "(?P<name>...) gives a group a name as well as a number, so code reads m.group('y') instead of m.group(1).",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "Expression",
       "Value"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "m.group('y')",
        "'2024'"
       ],
       [
        "m.group('m')",
        "'03'"
       ],
       [
        "m.groupdict()",
        "{'y': '2024', 'm': '03', 'd': '15'}"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        2,
        1
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Use groups to rearrange text",
     "text": "In re.sub, \\1, \\2, \\3 in the replacement stand for the groups. Here the order is reversed to day/month/year.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "Before",
       "After re.sub(pattern, r'\\3/\\2/\\1', text)"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Due 2024-03-15 and 2025-12-01",
        "Due 15/03/2024 and 01/12/2025"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        0,
        1
       ]
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "password",
   "title": "A password rule, one lookahead at a time",
   "topic": "6. Lookaround and flags",
   "blurb": "Each requirement is one lookahead; watch the wrong answers disappear.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Rule 1: at least 8 characters",
     "text": "Start with length: .{8,} means any character, 8 or more times. Highlighted cells are wrong answers: it also accepts PASSWORD, password1 and abcdefgh.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Passw0rdX",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "Abcdefg1",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "password1",
        "no",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "PASSWORD",
        "no",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "Ab1",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "abcdefgh",
        "no",
        "match"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        2,
        2
       ],
       [
        3,
        2
       ],
       [
        5,
        2
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Rule 2: it needs a digit (lookahead)",
     "text": "(?=.*\\d) is a lookahead: a check at the current position that looks ahead for 'anything, then a digit' and then consumes nothing. If the check passes, matching continues from the same place with .{8,}.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Passw0rdX",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "Abcdefg1",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "password1",
        "no",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "PASSWORD",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "Ab1",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "abcdefgh",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        2,
        2
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Rule 3: it needs an uppercase letter",
     "text": "A second lookahead, (?=.*[A-Z]), is added in front. Lookaheads do not consume characters, so all of them check the whole string from the start. Every row is now right.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Passw0rdX",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "Abcdefg1",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "password1",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "PASSWORD",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "Ab1",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "abcdefgh",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": []
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Reading the finished pattern",
     "text": "Each requirement is its own lookahead, so adding a rule is just adding a (?=...) and the order of the rules does not matter.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "Part",
       "Meaning"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "(?=.*\\d)",
        "somewhere ahead there is a digit"
       ],
       [
        "(?=.*[A-Z])",
        "somewhere ahead there is an uppercase letter"
       ],
       [
        ".{8,}",
        "then match 8 or more characters (this is the part that consumes the string)"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        2,
        1
       ]
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "email",
   "title": "Growing an email pattern",
   "topic": "8. Building complex patterns",
   "blurb": "Four stages, each fixing the failures of the one before.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Stage 1: the obvious shape",
     "text": "words, @, words, a dot, words: \\w+@\\w+\\.\\w+. It accepts the simplest address but rejects three real ones (highlighted): dots, + and - in names, a domain with several parts, and a hyphen in the domain.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "ann@example.com",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "j.smith+news@mail.co.uk",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "a_b-c@sub.domain.org",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "me@my-site.com",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "@example.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@example",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann example@x.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@@x.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@x..com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        1,
        2
       ],
       [
        2,
        2
       ],
       [
        3,
        2
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Stage 2: allow more in the name",
     "text": "Replace \\w+ before the @ with [\\w.+-]+, a set of word characters plus . + and -. Inside [] those three are literal. j.smith+news now works.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "ann@example.com",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "j.smith+news@mail.co.uk",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "a_b-c@sub.domain.org",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "me@my-site.com",
        "yes",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "@example.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@example",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann example@x.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@@x.com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ],
       [
        "ann@x..com",
        "no",
        "no match"
       ]
      ],
      "hl": [
       [
        1,
        2
       ],
       [
        2,
        2
       ],
       [
        3,
        2
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "title": "Stage 3: any number of domain parts",
     "text": "(?:\\.\\w+)+ means: a dot and a word, repeated one or more times. The (?:...) group is non-capturing: it groups without creating a numbered group. mail.co.uk and sub.domain.org now pass, but my-site.com does not.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
       "String",
       "Should match?",
       "This pattern"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "ann@example.com",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "j.smith+news@mail.co.uk",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
        "a_b-c@sub.domain.org",
        "yes",
        "match"
       ],
       [
